<Guide lang="en" slug="wintering-in-spain" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="sleutelbeheer" ctaTitle="Back to the UK, with your home in good hands?" ctaText="With key holding we check your home regularly and step in if anything goes wrong.">
  <h2 id="why">Why winter in the Axarquía</h2>
  <p>While Britain is grey and wet, the coast east of Málaga sees sunshine almost every day. In January and February daytime temperatures are usually 15 to 19 degrees, warm enough for lunch outside. The Axarquía is quieter than Benidorm or the western Costa del Sol, and the towns stay lived-in through the winter: the bakery, the market and the café stay open.</p>
  <p>For exact temperatures month by month, see <a href="/en/weather/">Axarquía weather and climate</a>, for example <a href="/en/weather/january/">the weather in January</a>.</p>
  <p>Popular places to winter are Nerja, Torrox Costa and Torre del Mar on the coast and, for walkers, Cómpeta and Frigiliana. Read more in <a href="/en/living/">living in the Axarquía</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="rules">The 90/180-day rule</h2>
  <p>Since Brexit, British citizens without Spanish residency can spend <strong>up to 90 days in any rolling 180-day period</strong> in the Schengen area, which includes Spain and most of the EU. The count looks back 180 days from each day you are there, and includes time spent in other Schengen countries. Your entries and exits are now recorded electronically under the EU’s Entry/Exit System, so overstaying is easily spotted and can lead to fines or an entry ban.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Three months in Spain over winter is fine, as long as you have not used up days in the months before.</li>
    <li>Splitting your time, for example six weeks before Christmas and six weeks in spring, is possible too, but count carefully.</li>
    <li>If you want to stay longer, you need a visa. See <a href="/en/guides/moving-to-spain-from-the-uk/">moving to Spain from the UK</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Staying under 90 days also keeps you clear of Spanish tax residency (183 days). As a non-resident owner you still pay annual non-resident income tax on your Spanish home.</p>

  <h2 id="health">Healthcare and insurance</h2>
  <p>With a UK Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) you can get necessary state healthcare during your stay. Take enough of your regular medication for the whole period, with a copy of your prescription, and check that your travel insurance covers a stay of several months and any pre-existing conditions.</p>

  <h2 id="home">Your own home or renting</h2>
  <p>Renting for three months in winter is very doable, and prices are lower outside the summer season. People who come back every year often buy an apartment or house. But that home then stands empty for eight or nine months a year, and needs looking after.</p>

  <h2 id="away">Your home when you are back in the UK</h2>
  <p>An empty house on the Costa del Sol has a hard time. Leaks after a storm, damp and mould when it is not aired, a pool that turns green, a power cut that defrosts the freezer, or unwelcome visitors. With nobody on site, you only find out when you come back.</p>
  <p>With <a href="/en/key-holding/">key holding</a> we check your home on a fixed schedule. After each visit you get photos and a short report. If something is wrong, we fix it once you agree. And before you arrive we get the house ready: aired, water and power on and, if you like, cleaned with some shopping in the fridge.</p>

  <h2 id="checklist">Checklist before you leave</h2>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>Water turned off at the stopcock, water heater off</li>
    <li>Fridge emptied and left open, or left running with someone checking</li>
    <li>Windows and shutters closed, air vents open against damp</li>
    <li>Valuables put away, alarm set</li>
    <li>Pool covered or maintenance arranged</li>
    <li>Garden irrigation on a timer or someone looking after it</li>
    <li>Key and contact details with your key holder</li>
    <li>Direct debits for IBI, water and electricity checked</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
